# =============================================================================
# ui/.prettierrc
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Purpose:
#   Prettier configuration for the CardDemo SPA. It fixes the formatting applied
#   to the TypeScript, TSX, JSON, YAML, HTML and Markdown files under ui/, and it
#   is the configuration resolved by the `format` (`prettier --check .`) and
#   `format:write` scripts in ui/package.json. The `format` script is a required
#   step in .github/workflows/ui-ci.yml, so these values are a build contract
#   rather than an editor preference: a value that disagrees with the committed
#   tree fails that job.
# Parameters:
#   Not applicable. This file declares formatter options and takes no inputs.
# Return values:
#   Not applicable. Configuration resolution produces no application value.
# Exceptions or errors:
#   Invalid YAML prevents configuration resolution; the format gate surfaces
#   that non-zero exit rather than ignoring it.
#
# WHY (non-obvious design decisions):
#   - Alternatives Considered: this file is YAML, not JSON. Prettier resolves an
#     extensionless `.prettierrc` as either, and JSON was rejected because strict
#     JSON admits no comments, which would move every rationale below away from
#     the option it explains. ui/package.json shows that cost directly: being
#     strict JSON, it has to collect its pin rationale in a separate
#     `carddemoDependencyRationale` key.
#   - Alternatives Considered: eslint-config-prettier and eslint-plugin-prettier
#     are deliberately absent, so formatting and linting stay independent checks.
#     Neither is in the pinned dependency set, and keeping them apart means a
#     formatting disagreement can never justify relaxing a `jsdoc/require-*` rule
#     in ui/eslint.config.js. That is safe because the ESLint config enables no
#     stylistic rule at all, so there is nothing for Prettier to contradict.
#   - Assumptions: seven of the nine values below equal Prettier's default in the
#     pinned version and are asserted anyway, because Prettier changes its own
#     defaults across major versions -- 2.0 changed trailingComma, arrowParens
#     and endOfLine, and 3.0 changed trailingComma again -- so an unset option
#     turns a dependency bump into a tree-wide reformat. An omitted option can
#     also still be supplied by a contributor's editor settings, which moves the
#     decision somewhere unreviewable rather than leaving it open.
#   - Trade-offs: the two values that do NOT track the default are printWidth and
#     singleQuote, and departing from the default is what earns them their place
#     here rather than what argues against it. An asserted default only guards
#     against a future Prettier changing its mind; an asserted non-default states
#     a decision this tree made. Adopting the pair reformatted 25 files in one
#     mechanical pass under the pinned prettier 3.9.6, which is a real cost and is
#     paid once: `format:write` performs it, `format` then holds it, and no
#     subsequent edit re-incurs it. It was accepted rather than avoided because
#     the alternative -- choosing the option that happened to match the tree as
#     first written -- lets whichever file was authored first set the house style
#     for the other 24, which is not a decision but an accident. Options whose
#     defaults suit this tree are left unset rather than asserted.
# =============================================================================

# Trade-offs: 100 rather than Prettier's default 80, for a reason specific to this
# tree and measured rather than assumed. Two things here are long by construction.
# Identifiers are traceable to the COBOL records they carry -- displayCardNumber,
# transactionCategoryBalance -- because that traceability is the point, and generic
# type arguments stack because keyset pagination is expressed as an envelope:
# `Promise<PageResponse<CardSummary>>` is 34 characters of return type before the
# parameter list. At 80 those combine to break a single typed signature across three
# lines; at 100 the same signature is one line. `listCards` in ui/src/api/cards.ts
# is the concrete case -- three lines before, one after -- and across five
# representative files the width removed 65 lines, 1524 down to 1459, entirely by
# rejoining signatures, import lists and generic call sites that carried no other
# reason to break.
# Trade-offs: the cost is that a reader in a narrow split view meets soft wrapping.
# That is a viewer setting each reader controls, whereas a hard wrap is committed
# into the diff for everyone -- which is the asymmetry that decides this.
# Alternatives Considered: 80, which is both Prettier's default and the width the
# tree was first written to, so it is the safer-SOUNDING choice. It lost because
# "the width the tree already uses" can only ever ratify whatever was authored
# first, which decides nothing. 120 was rejected in the other direction: it stops
# two files fitting side by side on a common display, and this tree is read as
# screen-plus-test pairs.
# Assumptions: no width can corrupt the verbatim message catalog in
# ui/src/messages/messages.ts, because Prettier never breaks a string literal. That
# file holds a 205-character line and passes the check unchanged at either width,
# which is measured evidence rather than an inference, and it is why a width change
# cannot put Transformation Rule T8's character-for-character message guarantee at
# risk.
printWidth: 100

# Assumptions: indentation is spaces so one level occupies the same width in every
# viewer. A tab renders at whatever tab stop the reader configured, which would
# make both the indent and the remaining printWidth budget viewer-dependent.
useTabs: false

# Assumptions: two spaces is the indent the committed tree uses, and with useTabs
# above the indent is exactly two characters rather than a tab-stop-dependent
# distance, which is what makes the printWidth budget mean the same to everyone.
tabWidth: 2

# Assumptions: semicolons are always printed to remove the automatic-semicolon-
# insertion hazard, where a statement beginning with `(` or `[` parses as a
# continuation of the previous line. Omitting them instead relies on Prettier
# inserting a defensive leading semicolon on exactly those lines.
semi: true

# Trade-offs: single quotes for TypeScript string literals, which is a departure
# from Prettier's default and from how this tree was first written. It is chosen to
# work WITH jsxSingleQuote below rather than on its own: this option governs
# JavaScript literals and that one governs JSX attribute values, so setting them to
# opposite sides makes the quote character carry information inside a .tsx file that
# mixes both on one line -- 8 files here do. A double quote is then always markup
# (`gap="large"`, `placeholder="Card number"`) and a single quote is always
# JavaScript (`useState('')`, `'next'`), so a reader parses which language a line is
# speaking before reading the line. Setting both to the same side, as the default
# does, erases that distinction; the cost is that neither option can be justified in
# isolation, which is why both comments cross-reference each other.
# Assumptions: this changes delimiters only and can never change a string's
# CONTENT. Prettier re-quotes a literal by swapping the delimiter and adjusting
# escapes, so a verbatim message constant survives byte-identical -- which is what
# makes a formatter setting safe to apply to a file whose whole purpose is
# character-for-character fidelity under Transformation Rule T8. The 25-file
# reformat this triggered was verified on exactly that point rather than assumed:
# the decoded string-literal multiset of all 26 TypeScript and JavaScript files was
# compared before and against after, and all 26 matched.
# Assumptions: no message constant needs escaping either way, which was measured
# rather than supposed -- ui/src/messages/messages.ts holds no literal containing an
# apostrophe, and none is expected, because the COBOL constants these transcribe are
# themselves apostrophe-delimited and a search of app/cpy finds zero doubled-quote
# escapes. So this option cannot introduce a backslash into a verbatim string, which
# is the one way a formatter could have made a message harder to read.
# Alternatives Considered: false, matching both the default and the tree as first
# authored. It lost for the reason recorded under printWidth -- deferring to what was
# written first ratifies an accident rather than deciding anything -- and because the
# JSX-versus-JavaScript signal above exists only on this side of the option.
singleQuote: true

# Assumptions: JSX attribute values stay double-quoted, matching ui/index.html, so
# a reader moving between the entry document and a component meets one convention
# for markup attributes.
# Trade-offs: this is deliberately the OPPOSITE side from singleQuote above, and the
# split is the decision rather than an oversight -- the two options are one choice
# and the reasoning is stated at singleQuote. JSX attribute syntax is HTML's, where a
# double-quoted attribute is the universal form; a TypeScript string literal is
# JavaScript's. Keeping each on its own language's convention is what lets the quote
# character distinguish them. Aligning JSX to the TypeScript side would additionally
# diverge every component from ui/index.html, which a reader meets first.
jsxSingleQuote: false

# Trade-offs: a trailing comma is printed wherever valid, which makes appending an
# entry to a multi-line list a one-line diff instead of two -- material for the
# long literal tables here, such as the message catalog and the theme token map --
# at the cost of a comma some readers find redundant. It is also the option
# Prettier has changed most, so it is the one most worth stating explicitly.
trailingComma: 'all'

# Trade-offs: a sole arrow-function parameter keeps its parentheses, so adding a
# type annotation or a second parameter changes only the text inside parentheses
# that already exist and the diff shows the change alone. The cost is slight noise
# on short callbacks; omitting them was Prettier's pre-2.0 default and was changed
# for this reason.
arrowParens: 'always'

# Assumptions: line endings are normalised to LF because this application is built
# and served on Linux. The concrete failure mode prevented is a file committed with
# CRLF from a Windows checkout: it looks correct locally and fails
# `prettier --check .` in CI. Fixing it at the formatter removes the class of
# problem rather than depending on every contributor's git autocrlf setting.
endOfLine: 'lf'
